## 3.1 $Z n_{4}\mathbf{O}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})_{6}$ SBU
The $Z{\mathsf{n}}_{4}0(\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{6}$ octahedron is undoubtedly one of the most widely used and studied SBUs in MOF chemistry, largely due to the extensive series of isoreticular MOFs (IRMOFs) of Yaghi and co-workers.35 The crystallographically determined structure of this cluster comprises four tetrahedral zinc ions coordinated to six carboxylate groups and linked by a central tetrahedral oxygen atom, $0_{\mathrm{cent}}$ (Fig. 1).
To calculate the structure of this SBU, we introduced two new atom types, namely Zn3f2 for describing the tetrahedrally coordinated $z_{n}!!$ ions and O_3_f suited for the central tetrahedral oxygen. In both notations “f” stands for atoms in framework materials. The new atom type Zn3f2 differs from the UFF standard $Z n3+2$ solely in the value of the bond radius, which is 0.05 $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ larger for the new atom type. However, this small change leads to significantly improved geometry of the $Z{\mathsf{n}}_{4}0(\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{6}$ SBU (see below). The $\mathsf{o}_{-^{3}-^{\mathsf{f}}}$ atom type has the same covalent radius as the UFF-defined $0\_2\ (r_{i}=0.634\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ , but differs from O_2 in bond angle $\langle\vartheta_{0}=109.47^{\circ}\rangle$ and coordination number (4), see Table 1. For the remaining atoms, we adopted standard UFF parameters (O_2 for carboxylic oxygen $0_{\mathsf{c a r b}}$ , C_R for carbon and ${\sf H}_{-}$ for hydrogen29). To reflect the bonding situation, we employed bond orders of $1\%$ for all ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{O}}_{\mathsf{c a r b}}$ , ½ for all $Z\mathsf{n}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{c a r b}}$ and $Z_{\mathsf{n-O_{c e n t}}},$ and 1 for all $C-H$ distances (Fig. 1a). The predicted structure of the $Z{\mathsf{n}}_{4}{\mathsf{O}}({\mathsf{C O}}_{2})_{6}$ SBU saturated with H atoms is depicted in Fig. 1b. The performance of the method is shown in Table 2 which compares the calculated and the experimental values of selected bond lengths and angles.
