## 3.2 Trimeric oxo-centered SBU
The trimeric oxo-centered assembly with the general formula $M_{3}O(C O_{2})_{6}$ has been experimentally realized in molecular complexes and/or framework compounds, with M corresponding to Al, Sc, V, Cr, Mn and Fe. The crystal structure of this cluster
## Journal of Chemical Theory and Computation
consists of three trivalent metal ions, $\mathsf{M}^{\sf{I I I}}$ , connected via six carboxylate groups and a central oxygen atom, $0_{\mathrm{cent}},$ with trigonal-planar configuration (see Fig. 2). The octahedral coordination environment of each $\mathsf{M}^{\sf{I I I}}$ site is completed by an axially coordinated capping ligand, typically a solvent molecule. For the sake of simplicity, the capping ligands are not considered in our SBU model structures. The $M_{3}O(C O_{2}H)_{6}$ cluster has an overall charge of $+1$ that was neglected in the UFF calculations. We justify our neglect of charge in the UFF calculation of the isolated SBU by noting that (i) UFF charges are only defined for intermolecular interactions and interactions between third and higher neighbours, (ii) the same SBU appearing in different frameworks will have its formal charge compensated for to differing degrees by the framework ligands and co-ordinated solvent. In addition, within a periodic framework, the extended geometry of the framework maintains the local symmetry of the SBU, regardless of the location of the counterion. On the other hand, in a calculation of an isolated SBU, the inclusion of an explicit counterion will break the symmetry of the SBU. Considering the SBU as uncharged is therefore likely to result in the most broadly applicable parameters.
To describe the octahedrally coordinated $\mathsf{M}^{\sf{I I I}}$ ions in the oxo-centered trimer, we introduced a set of new atom types, labeled $\mathsf{M}_{-}6{+}3$ , for $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{A l}^{\mathsf{I I I}},\mathsf{S c}^{\mathsf{I I I}},\mathsf{V}^{\mathsf{I I I}},\mathsf{M n}^{\mathsf{I I I}}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I I}}$ . For $C r^{111}$ , we used a slightly different notation, Cr6f3, to distinguish the new parameter from the UFF-existing $\mathsf{C r}6+3$ . The new atom type Cr6f3 differs from the UFF standard $\mathsf{C r}6+3$ solely in the value of the bond radius, which is 0.06 $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ shorter for the new atom type. This small modification of the Cr atom type improves the UFF predicted results only slightly in the case of the oxo-centered Cr trimer, but considerably in the case of the Cr-containing MIL53-type SBU (see Section 3.3). Each atom type from the M_ $6{+}3$ series (Table 1) is characterized by the bond angle of $90^{\circ}$ , coordination number of 6 and metal bond radius chosen with respect to a corresponding reference geometry. Additionally, for reflecting the trigonal-planar configuration of the central oxygen in $M_{3}O(C O_{2}H)_{6}$ that differs from the tetrahedral $0_{\mathsf{c e n t}}$ in the $Z{\mathsf{n}}_{4}0({\mathsf{C O}}_{2})_{6}$ SBU, we defined the O_2_z atom type with $r_{i}=0.528\textup{\AA},\vartheta_{0}=$ $120^{\circ}$ , and coordination number of 3 (Table 1). In this respect, O_2_z represents a hybrid of the UFF-defined O_2 and O_3_z atom types (cf. Table 1 in ref. 29). As in the case of the $Z{\mathsf{n}}_{4}0(\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{6}$ octahedron, the existing UFF O_2, C_R and ${\sf H}_{-}$ atom types were found to perform reasonably well for the carboxylic oxygens $(0_{\mathsf{c a r b}})$ , carbons and hydrogens in the oxo-centered trimer. The bonding situation was described using bond orders reflecting resonant ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{O}}_{\mathsf{c a r b}}$ bonds, single $Z n\mathrm{-}O_{\mathsf{c e n t}}$ and $C-H$ , and half $Z\mathsf{n}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{c a r b}}$ bonds (see Fig. 2a). The UFF predicted structure of the $M_{3}O(C O_{2}H)_{6}$ SBU is shown in Fig. 2b, and the calculated structural characteristics are presented in Table 3 along with the maximum percent errors with respect to the experimental reference data.
